<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>

    <!-- File-scoped namespaces enforced -->
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!--
      BARCH-003. `latest` meant "whatever rules the installed SDK happens to ship", and
      global.json asked for the newest SDK in existence including prereleases. Together with
      TreatWarningsAsErrors below, that is a build which fails whenever Microsoft ships a new
      rule, on a date nobody chose.

      It stopped being theoretical on 13 November 2026: a GitHub runner image picked up a
      .NET 11 prerelease, master went red with eight CA2026 errors, and no commit had been
      made. The same eight had been failing local Release builds for weeks, which is how the
      `-p:WarningsNotAsErrors=CA2026%3BCA2027` folklore started.

      Pinned to the feature band the platform targets. Adopting a newer analyzer surface is now
      a reviewable commit rather than a side effect of installing an SDK.

      global.json names one SDK: 10.0.404, latestPatch, no prereleases. docker/Dockerfile and
      the CI runner name the same one, and ToolchainArchitectureTests refuses a tree where the
      three disagree, so there is no longer a build path that chooses its own compiler.

      This arrived in three steps rather than one, and the order is the lesson. First the
      policy, because the prerelease was what broke master. Then the container and the runner,
      once MCR's tag API was read instead of guessed at: sdk:10.0 was serving 10.0.404 while CI
      built on 10.0.100 and a developer machine on 10.0.203, three feature bands for one commit.
      Only then this version, because naming it means every machine must have it, and that is a
      thing to ask for rather than to spring on somebody mid-build.
    -->
    <AnalysisLevel>10.0</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>Recommended</AnalysisMode>

    <!-- Treat warnings as errors in CI/Release -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      NuGet audit runs over the full transitive closure and fails Release on anything
      moderate or worse. B2.6 turned this on deliberately: the audit found eight
      high-severity advisories reaching the build through preview packages, and the only
      reason nobody noticed was that Debug does not promote warnings.
    -->
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>moderate</NuGetAuditLevel>

    <!-- Reproducible builds: same source in, byte-identical assembly out. -->
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

    <!-- Assembly info -->
    <Company>Diaspora</Company>
    <Product>Diaspora Platform</Product>
    <Copyright>Copyright © Diaspora $(CurrentYear)</Copyright>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      CA1707 — underscores in member names. Test methods read as sentences here.

      CA1711 — types ending in "EventHandler". The platform kernel established this
      naming with IDomainEventHandler in Phase B1, and "handler" is the term the CQRS
      pipeline uses throughout. Renaming to satisfy the rule would make the code less
      readable, not more.

      CA1873 — log arguments that "may be expensive". The analyzer flags any property
      access, which covers nearly every structured log call in the codebase. Where a call
      genuinely sits on a hot path (per-request token validation) an explicit
      ILogger.IsEnabled guard is used instead; suppressing the rule keeps the remaining
      once-per-operation call sites readable.

      CA1861 — constant array arguments. Raised only by EF Core's generated migration
      files, which are not hand-maintained.

      CA1000 — static members on generic types. This is the factory idiom the whole
      platform is built on: Result<T>.Success, PagedResult<T>.Create, Error.Validation.
      The BCL uses it in the same places (Task.FromResult, ImmutableArray<T>.Create), and
      the alternative — a non-generic companion class per result type — is strictly worse
      to read and to discover.

      CA1716 — type names matching reserved keywords in another CLR language. Raised for
      Result's companion type Error, which is a VB keyword. Renaming a type that appears
      in every handler signature in the platform to accommodate a language nothing here
      targets is not a trade worth making.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707;CA1711;CA1873;CA1861;CA1000;CA1716</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    B1.4. The platform's own analyzers, compiled from source and handed to every compilation in
    the repository.

    A ProjectReference rather than a package, because a package would need publishing before a
    rule could be tried and would let the rules drift from the platform they describe. The two
    metadata attributes are what turn a reference into an analyzer: OutputItemType hands the
    assembly to the compiler instead of the linker, and ReferenceOutputAssembly=false keeps
    Diaspora.Analyzers out of every project's reference set — nothing should be able to call it.

    Opt-out exists for exactly two cases and both are structural: the analyzer project cannot
    analyze itself, and the analyzer's test project references it as an ordinary library so it can
    instantiate the analyzers under test. Any other use of this switch is a rule being turned off
    for a whole project, which is what the .editorconfig in docs is for instead.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseDiasporaAnalyzers)' != 'false' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Diaspora.Analyzers'">
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src\Diaspora.Analyzers\Diaspora.Analyzers.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
